    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d550">

                <pb facs="0415" n="411" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg411"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/2161: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul General at Tientsin</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Caldwell</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-21T14:00:00-04:00">July 21, 1938—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>76. Your 128, July 18, 3 p.m.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d550-f1"/> If as stated in
                    the enclosure to your mail despatch No. 259 of March 25,<anchor n="27"
                        xml:id="d550-f2"/> Texas Company’s losses at Tehchow were the result of
                    looting “after Japanese occupied town”, the mere fact that as reported in your
                    despatch No. 293 of June 1<anchor n="27" xml:id="d550-f3"/>
                    <pb facs="0416" n="412" xml:id="pg412"/> such looting was done by bandits of
                    Chinese nationality would not necessarily relieve Japanese Government of
                    liability for those losses under the principles of responsibility set out in
                    Department’s 98 March 8, 6 p.m. to Hankow. It is suggested therefore that that
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">those</hi>] losses be reported to your Japanese
                    colleague as having been due to looting subsequent to Japanese occupation of the
                    town.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
